## 1. Introduction
Among the fiber composites [1], PBO fibers capture wide attention because their wonderful thermal stability [2] and mechanical properties [3]. As is known to all, the interfacial region [4], designated as ‘‘interphase” [5] between fiber and resin [6], acts as the key roles in load transferring [7] and determining the overall mechanical performance of advanced composites [8,9]. Owing to the inert surface of PBO fibers[10], the interface combination is weak and susceptible to generate microcracks or damages [11,12]. Because the formed microcracks often continue to derive and extend at interfacial region and eventually penetrate into the deep structure [13], the detection and restore are often laborious and costly [14]. More seriously, once the interfacial damage has developed [15], the overall properties and operation lifespan of the composites will be greatly compromised [16]. Efforts should be focused on exploring new self-healing strategy that allow for in situ repairing the interfacial failure of the composites to prolong their service life.
Up to now, extrinsic and intrinsic strategy are two main types of self-healing systems [17]. The extrinsic method generally comes into force by pre-introduced healing agent into materials [18]. The breaks and cracks existed in the materials will induce healing agents release and then perform the healing programme [19]. The pioneering group of White and Sottos [20] showed that the fabrication of autonomic repairing interface by attaching capsulesencapsulated healing agent and catalyst onto the surface of fibers [21]. Since then, more extrinsically interfacial self-healing techniques are gradually developed including the single-capsule system [18], capsule/dispersed catalyst system [21], phase-separated droplet/capsule system, double-capsule system, and all-in-one microcapsules system [17,21]. However, the healing numbers/- times of capsule/microcapsule are finite [22], hindering the application of extrinsic strategy in the repair of fiber reinforced composites [21]. Alternatively, intrinsic repair strategy realized by introducing reversible dynamic bonds such as Diels-Alder bonds [23], thiuram disulfide groups [24], alkoxyamine [25], coumarin units [26], acylhydrazone bonds [18], disulfide units [27], CAC bonds reshuffle with the aid of Ru-catalyzed [18], and hydrogen bonds [28] into the materials has been extensively applied [17]. Despite the intrinsic strategy involving the breakage and reformation of chemical bonds could be operated repeatedly [29], it still suffers from more or less severe drawbacks for instance much cost, complicated self-healing process and degraded mechanical properties of materials [21]. To overcome the above shortcomings, the self-healing materials induced by external stimulation [30], for example heating [31] and photo-irradiation [26], arise enormous interests among scientific researchers [30]. Meanwhile, the light with advantages of easily available, clean, inexpensive, unparalleled, remote activation and productive was especially captured much interest and has been greatly applied in self-healing materials [32,33]. Moreover, several self-healing interfaces in resin-based composites have been successfully constructed by anchoring the photothermal nano-agents including ${\sf A g}/{\sf C u}_{2}S$ nanoparticles[6], and PDA/BN nanosheets [34] on the PBO fiber surface in previous works of our group. Although some progresses have been made, finding a facile and valid strategy that can fabricate stable and multifunctional interface to overcome the potential defects existed in the PBO fiber and corresponding composites is still a great challenge.
Metal-organic frameworks [35], which is constituted by metal nodes and organic linkers [36], have shown huge potential in different fields for instance adsorption [35], catalysis [37] and sensing [38] result from high specific surface areas, excellent thermal and chemical stability, controllable pore sizes and extensive structural diversity [39,40]. Recently, several MOF materials constituted by metal/transition metal ions $\left(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\right.$ , $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ and $\ln^{3+}$ ) and benzene ring-containing organic linkers have been successfully introduced onto the PBO fibers and other fibers/fabrics via in situ growth methods in our previous publications [41] and few findable works [42,43], simultaneously enabling the fibers/fabrics with remarkably improved surface energies, roughness and anti-UV properties [41,44]. Hence, loading the suitable MOF nanocrystals can be regarded as a feasible and promising strategy to make up the potential defects (surface inertness, poor UV stability and weak combination with resin) of PBO fibers. Besides, the UV-adsorption properties of MOFs are mainly depending on the physical structures and chemical compositions of organic ligand components [42,45]. Generally, the MOF crystals bridged by organic linkers composed of pure/functional group-substituted benzene ring and carboxyl, such as terephthalic acid and 2-aminoterephthalic acid, will exhibit excellent absorbance in the UV or even visible regions. In the family of MOF materials, Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ [46] built by $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and 2- aminoterephthalic acid with the spindle-like topology [47,48], possesses wide and strong light response and adsorption in the wavelength ranging from 200 to $600~\mathrm{{nm}}$ [49], commendably covering the UV and visible light regions. Moreover, one more interesting fact is that the water-stable MOFs can act as efficient coagulants for sufficient heteroaggregation and destabilization of GO sheets from aqueous solutions, providing a cost-efficient and promising procedure to reduce the toxicity of GO in natural aquatic systems and fabricate the MOF-GO hybrids, which is mainly based on the comprehensive effects including hydrogen-bonding $\scriptstyle|\pi-\pi|$ acid-base interactions and electrostatic attraction (adjusting pH values) between MOF crystals and GO sheets [50]. Combining the above-mentioned discussions, we speculate that in situ growing well-defined Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals (chemical stability, UV–visible light adsorption and shielding) as the medium layer/coating to further introduce enough GO sheets (auxiliary UV–visible light adsorption and shielding, favorable NIR photothermal conversion) onto the surface of PBO fibers, could be a simple and feasible strategy to well resolve the known shortcomings of PBO fibers and construct stable multifunctional interfaces in composites.
As a proof of proposed conceive, an iron (III)-based MOF $\left(\mathrm{MIL}{-}88\mathrm{B-NH}_{2}\right).$ )-mediated coagulation strategy is developed in the present study to construct a stable and multifunctional (UV-resistance and repeatable self-healing) surface of PBO fibers. Specifically, the spindle-like/rod-shape Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are insitu formed onto carboxylated PBO fibers surface (PBO-COOH) in the first place by a well-known one-step hydrothermal method. Then, numerous micron-scale GO sheets are further immobilized onto the PBO fiber surface via the mediated effects of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals-the hydrogen-bonding $\scriptstyle{\left|\pi-\pi\right|}$ /acidbase (Lewis) interactions between them. As expected, the resulting composites fibers, denoted as PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO, present excellent UV-resistance properties and improved by $75\%$ compared with untreated PBO fibers which is result from the effect of synergetic adsorption and shielding/refection between Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals and coagulated GO sheets. Meanwhile, on the basis of favorable NIR photothermal conversion of introduced GO layers [51], PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO-TPU monofilament composites also display stable and repeatable self-healing interfaces, which is conducive to prolong the service life of PBO-related composites. On the whole, the developed MOF-mediated coagulation strategy in current work will potentially enable PBO and other high-performance fibers for valuable applications in sensors, functional fabrics, advanced composites and so on.
## 2. Methods
## 2.1. Materials
PBO fibers $12\ {\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ in diameter) were cleaned through Soxhlet extraction prior to use. Guangdong. Iron (III) chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99\%$ ), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{2}$ -BDC, $98\%$ , glacial acetic acid ( $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ , $99.7\%$ ), Pluronic F127 and ethanol $\mathrm{\CH}_{3}$ - $\mathrm{CH_{2}O H}$ , $95\%$ ) were obtained from Aladdin and Innochem Reagent Company.
## 2.2. Fabrication of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ fiber
PBO-COOH fibers were prepared refer to the method in previous literature [52]. The detailed fabrication process was as follows. First of all, iron (III) chloride hexahydrate $(0.892\ {\mathrm{g}},3.30\ \mathrm{mmol})$ was dispersed by $75\mathrm{mL}$ aqueous solution. Subsequently, $2\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ was added with continuous magnetic stirring for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Afterwards, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC $\left.0.3\mathrm{g},1.65\mathrm{mmol}\right].$ ) was added and stirred for another $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, the PBO-COOH fiber (about 0.1 g) was immersed in the above precursor solution and then jointly transferred into autoclave, maintaining at $120^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.24\mathsf{h}$ later, the obtained fibers were taken out when the temperature return to room temperature. The composites fiber was purified at $80^{\circ}C$ with fresh ethanol for $10\mathrm{{h}}$ . The PBO@Fe-MIL${88}\mathrm{B-NH}_{2}$ fiber was obtained after drying.
## 2.3. Fabrication of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -GO fiber
The GO sheets were synthesized by the modified means of Hummers. To fabricate the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fiber, the MOF-mediated coagulation strategy was applied in the present work. Specifically, the as-prepared PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.$ fiber $(20\mathrm{mg})$ was immersed into a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ , $100\mathrm{mg/L}$ of GO aqueous solution accompany with oscillating $100~\mathrm{rpm}$ , $25^{\circ}C$ , 24 h). Then, the obtained sample were rinsed with deionized water. Eventually, the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\mathrm{.NH}}_{2}$ -GO fiber was obtained after drying $(100\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,1\ \mathsf h)$ .
## 2.4. The interfacial self-healing evaluation of PBO fiber composites
PBO-TPU composite was fabricated according to the reported study [6]. The single fiber pull-out test was applied to get the debonding composites sample, and the values of interfacial shear strength (IFSS) were taken down. Then, the NIR $\cdot808\ \mathrm{nm},\ 2\ \mathrm{W}/$ $\mathrm{cm}^{2\cdot}$ ) light source was utilized to heal the debonding composite. SEM images was applied to directly observed weather or not the damage interfacial of composites was healed. The ratio of repaired and original IFSS value represent the self-healing efficiency (SE) of monofilament composite.
## 2.5. Mechanical and UV-resistance measurement
The tensile strength (TS) of modified PBO fibers was determined by single-fiber tensile test (Instron5500R, USA), which loading speed is $20\ \mathrm{mm/min}$ and more than 40 fibers were measured for the same sample. The TS values are calculated by using the method of the Weibull statistical. The monofilament pull-out test (model HM410) was utilized to evaluate the IFSS values of composites. More than 30 specimens need to be measured for each sample. The formula was as follows:
$$
I F S S={\frac{F_{\mathrm{max}}}{\pi d l}}
$$
Fmax represented the largest pull-out force (N), the diameter of PBO fibers $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ donated as d, the length of single fiber immersed in resin droplets $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ is labeled by l.
The UV-accelerated aging box $(960~\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{m}^{2}$ , $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $300{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ ) was used to assessed the UV-resistance test of modified PBO fibers. The irradiation distance is about $50\mathrm{cm}$ . The testing PBO fibers was attached on the glass slides using the tape. During the UV aging test, the fibers were continuously got out from the box at an interval of $48\mathrm{~h~}$ . Ultimately, the retention value of TS at certain irradiation time was made use of assessing the ultraviolet-resistance performance.
## 3. Results and discussion
As has been noted, the high-performance PBO fibers are still faced with several deficiencies including surface inertness [52], vulnerable to UV light irradiation and poor binding affinity with resin matrix [41] seriously limiting the application of PBO fibers. Meanwhile, the improved methods reported so far remain unsatisfactory and the main shortages are as follows: complex preparation process, inhomogeneous modification effects and low coverage of UV-protection materials. Under this background, owing to the merits of MOF nonomaterials including diverse geometric topologies and preparation methods [53,54], easy to nucleate and grow on various substrates, excellent stability [55] and optical response [56], it have become the alternative and promising UV-protection/blocking coating on fibers/fabrics [41]. Moreover, the stable MOF nonocrystals also can act as the novel coagulants [50] to immobilize enough GO nonosheets onto the surface of PBO fibers, which well solve the problems of previously reported methods such as chemical grafting [12,57] (uneven and low loading) and layer-by-layer self-assembly [58] (complicated and tedious process, incomplete and instable coverage). In view of the advantages of MOFs and GO, herein, the strategy of MOFmediated coagulation is developed. The Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with outstanding absorbance in the UV–visible light regions was selected as the targeted MOF material and in-situ grown onto the PBO-COOH surface via hydrothermal method. Then, numerous GO nonosheets with NIR photothermal conversion abilities are further immobilized on the surface of PBO fibers by oscillation. Finally, the composites fiber labeled as PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO with multifunctional features (uvioresistant, repeatable self-healing interface in composite) are obtained. Fig. 1 illustrated the preparation process of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -GO.
## 3.1. Surface morphology and structure characterization
The SEM is used to capture the surface morphologies of fibers. As shown in Fig. 2a-c, the surface of pristine PBO fibers is smooth and neat (Fig. 2a), while the surface of chemically treated fibers exhibit several narrow grooves along the axial direction owing to the etching of the acid and alkali reagents $\mathrm{\Delta(H_{2}S O_{4}}$ , NaOH, $\mathrm{ClCH}_{2}$ - COOH, Fig. 2b–c) [23]. As for the $\mathrm{PBO@Fe-MIL-88B-NH_{2}}$ fiber (Fig. 2e, f), the surface status becomes more rougher with a dense Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocoating evenly anchored on the outside, which is benefitted from the additional nucleation sites on surface of PBO-COOH fibers. Meanwhile, the 3D structure diagram of FeMIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ visualized along the c axis with components of iron, oxygen, nitrogen and carbon atoms is depicted in the inset of Fig. 2f. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is used to determine the contents or weight percent of Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals on the surface of PBO-COOH fibers. As shown in Fig. S1a, the weight percent of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals on the surface of PBOCOOH fibers was about $45~\mathrm{mg/g}$ (4.5 wt%) after calculation based on the weight loss values of PBO-COOH and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ fibers at $998^{\circ}C$ . Moreover, in order to further get the detailed information of the Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocoating, the spindle-like Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals, which exhibit $80-100~\mathrm{~nm}$ in diameters and $400{\-}600\ \mathrm{nm}$ in lengths (Fig. 2d), are successful obtained by hydrothermal treatment $120^{\circ}\mathsf C,24\mathrm{h}^{\prime}$ . The XRD spectrum (Fig. S2) and TG pattern (Fig. S3) also consistently demonstrate the successful synthesis of phase-pure Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals [46,48]. After immobilization with GO sheets, the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO fiber (Fig. 2g, h) exhibits an obviously wrinkled and undulant surface morphology, on which the outer compact GO layers well wrap up the inner Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ coating. The loading amount of GO sheets on the $\operatorname{PBO@}$ Fe-MIL-88B$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ fibers was acquired based on the absorbance changes of GO solution at $234~\mathrm{nm}$ before and after coagulation. As shown in Fig. S1b, the content of GO sheets on the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ fibers were about $3.61~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , which was equivalent to $3.77~\mathrm{mg/g}$ $({\sim}0.38~\mathrm{wt\%})$ when compared to the PBO-COOH fibers. Moreover, local aggregation also occurs in Fe-MOF because of the twodimensional (2D) large-plane structures of GO sheets, reflecting the strong interactions between them. In addition, the sheetsheet $\scriptstyle{\left|{\pi-\pi}\right.}$ stacking interaction of GO also contributes to the formation of tight GO layers [44]. Therefore, the SEM results effectively confirm the feasibility of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -mediated GO coagulation strategy. The detailed calculation methods and similar result also can be seen in Fig. S4 in supporting information.
The FTIR and Raman spectra depicted in Fig. 3 were applied to determine the surface functional moieties and chemical structures of samples. As demonstrated in Fig. 3a, the unique peaks of $-c{-}0{-}C$ , $-C=\Nu$ and the vibrations of aromatic $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ stretching are presented in all of the PBO fibers, which are positioned centrally in 1056, 1628 and $3000{-}3100~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively [12]. With regard to the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber, a new typical peak located around $1726~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ emerges, which represents $-0-0=0$ vibrations. Besides, the peak intensities of $-c{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bonds are visibly enhanced in contrast with untreated PBO fiber. Meanwhile, there are three-adjacent strong bonds at about 1580, 1480 and $1395~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ existed in the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber, belonging to the deprotonated ACOOH groups stretching vibrations. Moreover, the unique vibrations of Fe3- ${\cdot\upmu3}$ -oxo clusters and ${\mathrm{-OH/NH}_{2}}$ groups located approximately at 621 and 3300– $3600~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ also appear on the composites fiber [46]. The above results combined with the FTIR spectrum of Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals (Fig. S5) collectively confirm the successful fabrication of $\mathrm{PBO@Fe-MIL-88B-NH_{2}}$ fiber by hydrothermal treatment. After coagulation of GO sheets, the characteristic curve of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber is basically similar to that of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ fiber in comparison. But, the vibration intensities of ${\mathrm{-OH/NH_{2}}}$ and aromatic $-C-H$ groups are further strengthened and the characteristic peak of Fe3- ${\cdot\upmu3}$ -oxo clusters is almost disappeared, all of which can be ascribed to the $-0\mathsf{H}$ groups, water vapor adsorption, aromatic conjugated structure of densely covered GO sheets.
To further demonstrate the successful preparation of hybrid fibers, the Raman patterns are recorded. As illustrated in Fig. 3b, the PBO-Desized fiber merely exhibits a wide and gradually upward adsorption with no identifiable characteristic peaks in the whole tested shift range. This circumstance is also reflected in the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber, which could be in relation to the organic compositions (fluorescence feature) of PBO fiber and Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ nanocrystals. By comparison, the PBO@Fe-MIL${88}\mathrm{B-NH}_{2}$ -GO fiber presents two clear and strong adsorption peaks at 1360 and $1590\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the D and G characteristic signals of carbon materials [58] and confirming the immobilization of GO sheets on the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber surface. In addition, the peak intensity ratio means the disorder degree of modified fiber [44], which is 0.81 $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ after calculation, indicating the favorable retention of electronic conjugation state of GO after coagulation process. In general, the analysis of FTIR and Raman spectra similarly attests the successful preparation of hybrid fibers in agree with the foregoing SEM results.
## 3.2. UV–Vis spectra analysis
The optical absorption abilities and potential UV-resistance properties of specimens are assessed by UV–Vis spectra test. The pristine PBO fiber demonstrates a strong and broad adsorption in the UV light region $(240-400\ \mathrm{nm})$ ), which is the main reason of molecular structure destroying and mechanical properties greatly degrading under the irradiation of UV light [44], while the absorbance in visible $400{\-}750\ \mathrm{nm},$ ) and NIR regions (over $750~\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{.}$ is relatively weak and gradually decrease (Fig. 4a). Instead, the absorbance values of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ fiber is observably strengthened weather UV or visible regions, which are originated from the excellent optical absorptivity of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals [59] (Fig. S6). Furthermore, the adsorption intensities of PBO@Fe-MIL${88}\mathrm{B-NH}_{2}$ -GO fiber shows more obvious enhancement trend in the whole UV–vis region compared to those of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ fiber because of the synergistic absorbance of Fe-MIL-88B$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ nanocrystals and densely loaded GO sheets. More importantly, an increased and effective NIR absorption is further witnessed in the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber, which can be primarily ascribed to the favorable NIR light responsiveness of GO sheets [44]. All the above results received from solid UV–Vis spectra test suggest that it is expected to construct the PBO fiber/resin composite with multifunctional interface (uvioresistant, self-healing) by take advantage of the synergism of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals and GO sheets (UV–vis adsorption and shielding, NIR photothermal conversion).
## 3.3. The interfacial performance evaluation
The interfacial performance of fiber composites is highly dependent on the wettability, compatibility and mechanical interlock [53,60]. To assess the interfacial compatibility, the surface energies of pristine and composites PBO fibers are measured through dynamic contact angle test (DCAT) [59] The collected testing data containing dispersive component $(\gamma^{\mathrm{d}})$ , polar component $(\gamma^{\mathsf{p}})$ and total surface energy $(\gamma^{t})$ are summarized and depicted in Fig. 4b. As demonstrated, the PBO-Desized fiber has the lowest surface energy of $42.04~\mathrm{mN/m}$ with the $\gamma^{\mathrm{d}}$ and $\gamma^{\mathrm{p}}$ values of 37.5 and 4.54 $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{mN/m}}$ . The smooth and inert cortex of pristine PBO fiber led to the poor surface feature. After modification, a significant promotion occurs in the surface energy of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber, and the detailed values of $\gamma^{\mathtt{d}},\gamma^{\mathtt{p}}$ and $\gamma^{t}$ are separately 48.26, 9.39 and $57.65~\mathrm{\mN/m}$ . The increase $(37.13\%)$ in surface energy of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber compared to pristine fiber is mainly ascribed to the surface polarity $(\gamma^{\mathsf{p}}$ contribution) and spindle-like topology $(\gamma^{\mathrm{d}}$ contribution) of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystal. In other words, the enhancement of surface roughness can bring about the obvious improvement of surface energy of PBO fibers especially the $\gamma^{\mathrm{d}}$ component value. As for the $\operatorname{PBO@}$ Fe-MIL-88B$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber, the surface energy $(\gamma^{t})$ is further increased to $63.32~\mathrm{mN/m}$ and the values of $\gamma^{\mathrm{p}}$ and $\gamma^{\mathrm{d}}$ components are 18.36 and $44.96~\mathrm{mN/m}$ , respectively. In contrast, the surface energy of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber is increased by $50.62\%$ compared to pristine PBO fiber due to the high surface polarity (more $\gamma^{\mathsf{p}}$ contribution) of coagulated GO layers.
## 3.4. The mechanical performance studies
The tensile and pull-out tests of monofilament are performed to estimate the mechanical performance of PBO fibers (Fig. 4c). In contrast to pristine PBO fiber (4.93 GPa), the TS of PBO@Fe-MIL${\mathsf{88B-N H}}_{2}$ and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO fibers are gradually decreased to 3.62 and $3.1\mathsf{G P a}$ , respectively. Nevertheless, the TS values of hybrid fibers are still relatively satisfactory according to the reported literatures [7,41]. The decline of TS values for hybrid fibers is ascribe to the negative effects of acidified and carboxylated, hydrothermal and coagulated treatment processes. Among them, the etching effects of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ , $_\mathrm{ClCH_{2}C O O H}$ and NaOH treatment are more influential with several defects (grooves) appeared in the surface of PBO fibers, causing the degradation of mechanical performance. Besides, the aging effects of long-time $(24\ \mathrm{~h~})$ hydrothermal growth and coagulation processes are also responsible for the TS loss (small) of hybrid fibers. As expected, the changes of interfacial shear strength (IFSS) values exhibit an opposite trend. The IFSS value of pristine PBO fiber composite is only $51.31~\mathrm{MPa}$ . By comparison, the IFSS value of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber composite $(72.24\ \mathrm{MPa})$ shows a striking enhancement increased by $40.74\%$ , indicating the fine interfacial combination and mechanical interlock between PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber (improved surface energy and roughness) and matrix. But, after absorption of GO, IFSS value of composite decline to $68.12~\mathrm{MPa}$ , which is $32.55\%$ higher than PBO-Desized composite. The possible interpretation is the relatively weakened surface roughness of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO fiber, which is in connection with the potential ‘‘lubrication effect” of covered micron-scale GO sheets. On the whole, the results of Fig. 4b and 4c demonstrate that the proposed MOF-mediated strategy is feasible to improve the performance of PBO fiber and corresponding composite.
## 3.5. The photothermal properties measurements
The $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ NIR laser is selected as the irradiation light source with the power of $2{\mathsf{W}}/{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}$ to assess the photothermal conversion properties of samples. First of all, the photothermal conversion capacity of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with various concentrations (0/pure water, 1, 2 and $5~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ ) is estimated. The temperature changes are recorded by thermal imager shown in Fig. S7. As measured, there is no obvious rise in temperature of $\mathrm{Fe-MIL-}88\mathrm{B-NH_{2}}$ dispersions even after $5\mathrm{min}$ irradiation. Based on this, the surface temperature changes of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ fiber are further determined within $5~\mathrm{min}$ irradiation (interval, one minute). The fiber samples are placed flat on the slide with both ends fixed by double-sided tape. The surface temperatures of PBO@Fe-MIL$88\mathrm{B-NH}_{2}$ fiber (Fig. 5a, c) present a growth tendency from fast to slow with the values rising from 25 to $74^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ The higher photothermal conversion capacity of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ nanocrystals anchored on PBO fiber than in aqueous solution can be explained as the outstanding light-gathering and absorbing performance in solid aggregation status. After GO coagulation, the surface temperature values of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber exhibit the faster increase from 25 to $198^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ than PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ fiber only within oneminute irradiation time at an interval of $10~s$ (Fig. 5b and d). The prominent photothermal conversion efficiency of PBO@Fe-MIL${88}\mathrm{B-NH}_{2}$ -GO fiber is attribute to the strong sheet-sheet $\scriptstyle{\frac{}{}}\pi-\pi$ interactions of GO. The above results in accordance with Fig. 4a confirm that the composites fibers, especially the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ - GO fiber, possess excellent NIR photothermal conversion capacity benefitted from the cooperation effects of densely covered GO layers (major) and well-grown Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals (minor).
## 3.6. Interfacial self-healing analysis
The IFSS test, NIR light source $\mathrm{\Delta}808\mathrm{\nm},2\mathrm{\DeltaW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ) and SEM technique are applied to affirm the interfacial self-healing behavior of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fiber composite. The thermoplastic polyurethane (TPU) with light transmittance, low Tg value (around $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and melting point is chosen as the resin matrix [6]. The prepared composites are denoted as PBO-TPU (control group) and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU, respectively. As shown in Fig. 6, the interphases of original PBO-TPU (Fig. 6a) and PBO@FeMIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU (Fig. 6d) composites are intact. After IFSS test, the microcracks are existed in the interfaces of PBO-TPU (Fig. 6b) and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -GO-TPU (Fig. 6e) composites. However, the surface circumstances of pristine and composites fibers are obviously different after de-bonding treatment. To be specific, the exposed surface of pristine PBO fiber accompanied with TPU resin removal is still clean and smooth and the fracture notch at the interfacial position is relatively complete. By comparison, interfacial fracture of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite is more serious and irregular with more microcracks and several TPU resin fragments emerged at the de-bonding region. These results indicate the favorable interfacial combination and mechanical interlock between $\mathrm{PBO@Fe-MIL-88B-NH_{2}-G O}$ fiber and TPU resin. After irradiated by the $808\ \mathrm{\nm}$ NIR laser for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , there is no discernable change is observed in PBO-TPU composite (Fig. 6c), implying the poor and negligible repair properties of PBO-TPU composite. Contrarily, the debonding PBO@FeMIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite is well repaired (Fig. 6f). Because the Tg value of TPU resin is higher than the maximum surface temperature of $\mathsf{P B O@F e-M I L-88B-N H_{2}}$ fiber after NIR laser irradiation (Fig. 5c), therefore, the interfacial self-healing performance of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite is mainly profited from the excellent NIR photothermal conversion capacity of coagulated GO sheets. Moreover, the morphology and status of composite are almost unchanged before and after NIR irradiation, suggesting the fine stability of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fiber and TPU resin towards NIR laser.
## 3.7. Photo-thermal self-healing mechanism
The possible self-healing mechanism/process of PBO@Fe-MIL88B- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite can be described as follows. As schemed in Fig. 7, the originally prepared PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - GO-TPU composite with intact interface is firstly subjected to the action of external force during the IFSS measurement, resulting in the debonding and failure of interface. Then, the $808~\mathrm{{nm}}$ NIR laser is exerted to trigger the repair behaviors of PBO@Fe-MIL88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite based on the notable photothermal conversion performance of immobilized dense GO layers. The surface temperature of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber is gradually increase with the extension of NIR laser irradiation time. Once reached and exceeded the Tg value of TPU resin, the TPU droplet start to melt and flow due to the thawing and freely moving of internal segments. After that, the flowing TPU resin arrives at the debonding interfacial position, re-contacts the exposed surface of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber and redistributes at the interfacial fracture notch of composite. Later, a new stable interface is formed at the broken position after $60\ \mathrm{min}$ NIR irradiation. Finally, the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite realizes the interfacial repair and reconstruction because of the excellent NIR light-toheat conversion capacity of GO sheets and the strong mechanical interlock between residual Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO coating and TPU resin.
## 3.8. Interfacial self-healing properties evaluation
The self-healing efficiency (SE) of PBO fibers composites is used as an index to evaluation the self-healing properties. As is shown, the debonding PBO-TPU composite (Fig. 8a) just shows low and little unchanged SE values that fluctuate between $15.2\%$ and $21.4\%$ , reflecting the untreated PBO fiber without the NIR light adsorption and conversion performance [34]. However, the SE values of debonding PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite represents an obviously different and enhanced growth trend under the identical NIR treatment conditions, indicating the potentially outstanding interfacial repair performance. Notably, the maximum SE value of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO-TPU composite can come up to $81.3\%$ after $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ NIR irradiation, further authenticating the prominent NIR photothermal conversion ability of dense GO layers and corresponding well with the self-healing mechanism in Fig. 7. Besides, the changes of SE values for composites under NIR irradiation over 5 cycles are also investigated and displayed in Fig. 8b. The irradiation time of per cycle is $60\mathrm{{min}}$ with the power of $2\mathsf{W}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . As can be seen, the SE values of PBO-TPU composite still remain low level ranging from $14.3\%$ to $22.9\%$ , which is similar to the results in Fig. 8A. By comparison, PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU composite exhibits a relatively stable variation of SE values in the range of $78.9\substack{-81.3\%}$ during the 5 cycle times, suggesting the robust and repeatable interfacial self-healing properties. In summary, the total results of thermal images, SEM/IFSS tests and SE values strongly confirm that PBO fiber reinforced TPU composite can achieve stable, valid and repeatable intrinsic repair with the irradiation of NIR laser based on the proposed Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -mediated GO sheets coagulation strategy.
## 3.9. UV-resistance performance measurements
The UV-resistance performance of pristine and composites fibers is also explored by the tensile strength values of PBODesized, PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fibers at different irradiation time periods (overall: $288{\mathrm{~h~}}$ ; interval: $48\mathrm{h}$ ). The TS values of all samples gradually decrease with the prolongation of UV irradiation time (Fig. 9a). However, in contrast to the original PBO fiber, the difference is that the loss/descent rates of TS values for composites fibers are apparently low. Moreover, the TS of composites fibers is even higher than PBO-Desized fiber after $96\mathrm{h}$ , despite having the lower initial TS values. The above satisfactory results indicate that the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fibers possess potentially outstanding uvioresistant performance. To compare the UV-resistance performance of original and composites fibers in a quantitative manner, the retention percentages of TS values at various UV aging time periods are calculated and selected as the important estimation index. From the Fig. 9b, the TS retention values of all samples exhibit a gradual downward trend when the aging time increase from
$48\mathrm{h}$ to $288{\mathrm{h}}$ . Nevertheless, in comparation with original PBO fiber, the TS retention values of composites fibers maintain large values. Notably, the retention percentages of TS value for PBO@Fe-MIL${\mathsf{88B-N H}}_{2}$ and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO fibers after $96\mathrm{h}$ UV irradiation still can arrive at $92\%$ and $94\%$ , which are remarkably superior to that of pristine PBO fiber $(60\%)$ . In addition, the TS retention values at $288{\mathrm{~h~}}$ aging time are used to eventually evaluate the UV-resistance performance of samples. The TS retention percentages of original PBO fibers is $32\%$ . Meanwhile, the TS retention percentages of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B$\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fibers are separately $50\%$ and $56\%$ after $288{\mathrm{~h~}}$ irradiation time, demonstrating excellent anti-UV performance. After calculation, the TS retention values of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fibers are increased by $56.25\%$ and $75\%$ , respectively. That is to say, after absorption of GO, the UVresistance performance of fiber is enhanced. The synergistic UVprotection effects (enhanced adsorption and shielding attenuation/reflection) of well-grown Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanocrystals and densely covered GO sheets make the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fiber with more outstanding anti-UV properties.
It is known that PBO fiber has a typical skin-core structure characteristic [41,44] in which the skin/cortex acts a critical part in the inherent mechanical performance of fiber [41]. Generally speaking, the degradation process of PBO fiber induced by UV light irradiation can be grouped to physical and chemical degradation [44]. The PBO fiber firstly undergo the physical degradation stage such as at $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ UV irradiation time, then with the extension of aging time (for example at $96\mathrm{~h~}$ ), the chemical degradation stage will happen. Among them, the substantial loss of TS value primarily occurs in the chemical degradation stage result from the serious destruction of molecular chains in the cortex and the consequent decrease of crystallinity of PBO fiber (forming lots of slender fringes on the surface). Therefore, the TS and retention values of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO fiber after irradiation for $96\mathrm{~h~}$ are used to compare with other reported PBO fibers under the less $^{\prime30\mathrm{~h~}}$ , $70\mathrm{{h}}^{\cdot}$ )/same $(96~\mathrm{h})/\mathsf{a}$ little more $(100\mathrm{h})$ aging time. Fig. 10a represented the comparison results of UV-resistance performance. Significantly, the TS value (2.91 GPa) and retention percentage $(94\%)$ of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fiber at $96\mathrm{~h~}$ degradation time are still satisfactory among the reported literatures and in close proximity to the optimum performance area, where is defined as the TS and retention values are equal/greater than 3 GPa and $90\%$ , respectively. Besides, the TS retention value of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fiber is basically at the highest level after comparison no matter what the degradation time among the literatures. Moreover, as can be seen, only few literatures (RS7, RS8) exhibit the data at the boundary of optimum area and the TS values are higher than that of $\mathrm{PBO@Fe-MIL-88B-NH_{2}-G O}$ fiber. However, the irradiation source is xenon lamp ${\mathit{\Omega}}\left(\lambda=300{-}800\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ ) in these literatures and the degradation degree is weaker than that of pure UV irradiation $(300{-}500\mathrm{nm},960\mathrm{W}/\mathrm{m}^{2})$ conducted in the present work. From the above-mentioned aspects, therefore, the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ - GO fiber fabricated via in situ hydrothermal MOF growth and follow-up MOF-mediated GO coagulation strategy possess prominent UV-resistance properties that are superior to most of the literatures reported so far.
## 3.10. Anti-UV mechanisms
To elucidate the excellent uvioresistant performance of composites fibers, the possible anti-UV mechanisms are showed in Fig. 10b. The original PBO fiber is vulnerable to UV light irradiation for long time because of the oxazole rings units, causing the serious destruction of molecular structure and substantial loss of TS value on the macro level. The detailed UV-aging degradation process can be divided into two stages. First of all, the oxazole ring opening reaction is occurred in the benzoxazole structure of PBO fiber, leading to the formation of intermediate product containing amide bonds. Then, the amide bonds of intermediate product continue to break/cleave with the irradiation time going on. As a result, the original monomers including 4,6-diaminoresorcinol (DAR) and terephthalic acid (TA) are regenerated and the molecular chains of PBO fiber are completely broken. As presented in Fig. 4a, the UV–vis absorbance of $\mathrm{PBO@Fe-MIL-88B-NH_{2}}$ fiber is enhanced compared with untreated PBO fiber, which is originated from the wide and strong optical absorptivity of Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the UV–vis regions. After immobilization with GO sheets, the UV–vis absorbance is further promoted owing to the synergistic effects of Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ nanocrystals and densely covered GO layers. Meanwhile, the adsorbed UV–vis light will be converted into heat and emitted into the surrounding environment, leading to the surface temperature increase of composites fibers. In addition, the well-grown Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ nanocrystals and coagulated GO sheets separately with spindle-like and 2D planar morphology also acts a critical part in shielding attenuation/reflection of UV irradiation. Consequently, the intensity and energy of UV light is largely weakened until reach the deepest surface layer of PBO fiber and degrade the chemical structure will obviously reduce compared to untreated PBO fiber. The above proposed UV-resistance mechanisms commendably explain the outstanding anti-UV properties of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO fiber and powerfully support the results of UV–Vis DRS and TS retention tests, which are superior to those of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ and pristine fibers.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, one-dimensional (1D) versatile PBO@Fe-MIL${88}\mathrm{B-NH}_{2}.$ -GO fiber is achieved by hydrothermal treatment and MOF-mediated coagulation strategy. Herein, numerous GO sheets can be simply and effectively immobilized onto the surface of PBO fiber due to the hydrogen-bonding $\scriptstyle|\pi-\pi|$ acid-base (Lewis) interactions between Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ nanocrystals and GO sheets. The PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- ${\cdot\}\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -GO-TPU monofilament composite exhibited stable interfacial self-healing properties. More importantly, the SE after 5 cycle times can still maintain nearly $80\%$ . Besides, the PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO fiber, which is irradiated by UV for $288{\mathrm{~h~}}$ , also exhibited optimum UV-resistance properties increased by $75\%$ compare with untreated PBO fibers. Furthermore, the uvioresistant performance of PBO@Fe-MIL-88B- $\cdot\Nu\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -GO fiber at $96\mathrm{~h~}$ irradiation time is also superior to much of literatures reported so far and close to the optimum level after comparison. The multifunctional surface/interface (UV-resistance, repairable) of PBO fiber and corresponding composite is attributed to the excellent NIR photothermal conversion capacity of densely covered GO layers and the synergistic adsorption, shielding attenuation/reflection effects of Fe-MIL-88B- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ -GO hybrid coating. Based on the varieties and multifunctionalities of MOFs and carbon materials, the strategy of MOF-mediated coagulation will potentially enable PBO and other high-performance fibers for valuable applications in sensors, functional fabrics, advanced composites and so on.
